Sebastian Hungerecker <sepp00@web.de> writes:
> If so, exactly when would using a game play altering patch not be
> playing a variant but actual cheating?

In any context where you're expected to be or claiming to be playing
nethack. Specific examples would be YAAP on the 'froup (unless clearly
noted as involving a variant), dicksizing on the basis of score,
conducts, speed or any other metric, or exchanging bones via Hearse.

Variants -- meaning any patch which impacts play -- aren't the same
game. That's fine, but you can't directly compare your results to
those of people playing vanilla.

Given that some of the earlier nethack releases (I'm thinking 2.x)
were vastly easier (IMHO) to ascend, I'd also consider it a bit dodgy
for someone today to claim to have "beaten nethack" if they'd
deliberately dredged up some archaeological version from ages past for
the purpose.

Playing on a single-player system is solitaire. What constitutes
cheating at solitaire is between you and your own conscience, since
the only possible victim is yourself.


